# Getting Started

## Overview

You'll build a support Agent that looks up an order and answers the customer.
The model runs offline, so the notebook needs no API key. By the end, this
works:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, next_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

reply.data.output
# => "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02."
```

The parts:

| Part | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| AI Agent | A Jido Agent with one root reasoning Flow and a list of tool Actions or Flows. |
| Reasoning Flow | The graph that calls the model and runs tools. This guide uses ReAct. |
| Tool | A Jido Action or Flow the model can call by name. |
| Session | The conversation, stored in Agent state at `state.session`. |

ReqLLM sends the model calls. Here it sends them to a local test plug instead
of a provider.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

`plug` provides `Req.Test`, which the offline model uses.

## Define A Tool

A tool can be a Jido Action or Flow. The model sees its `name`, `description`, and
parameter schema:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/actions/lookup_order",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the shipping status of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema:
      Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), status: Zoi.string(), eta: Zoi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end
```

## Declare The Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
      instructions "You answer customer-support questions. Look up orders before you answer."

      limits do
        model_calls 4
        tool_rounds 2
      end
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end
  end
end
```

| Line | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `use Jido.AI.Agent` | Defines a Jido Agent with a `session` state field and a `jido.ai.request` route. |
| `reasoning` | Selects the root Flow. An Agent has exactly one. |
| `model`, `instructions`, `limits` | Request defaults, checked against the ReAct input schema at compile time. |
| `tools` | The Agent's tool allowlist. A request can narrow it but can't add to it. |

A default that the Flow schema rejects fails the build. `model_calls 40`
(the ReAct maximum is 16) raises `reasoning configuration is invalid for
Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct at limits.model_calls`.

## Inspect The Agent

Three generated functions read the declaration without calling a model:

```elixir
MyApp.SupportAgent.reasoning()
```

The result is `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct`.

```elixir
explained = MyApp.SupportAgent.explain()

{explained.tools, explained.defaults.model, explained.defaults.limits.model_calls}
```

The result is `{[MyApp.LookupOrder], "openai:gpt-4o-mini", 4}`. `explain/0` also
returns the Flow's `input_schema`, `output_schema`, and compiled `flow`.

`preflight/1` validates one request and compiles the root Flow. It doesn't run
it:

```elixir
{:ok, preflight} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?"})

{preflight.params.limits.tool_rounds, preflight.params.options}
```

The result is
`{2, %{receive_timeout: 30000, max_tokens: 128, temperature: 0.0}}`. Fields you
didn't declare take the ReAct schema defaults.

An invalid request returns the schema error:

```elixir
{:error, error} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{})

Exception.message(error)
```

The result is `"is required, at input"`.

## Offline Model

This cell is the fake provider. It answers ReqLLM's HTTP requests with
OpenAI Responses API JSON, in the order you queue them:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.OfflineModel do
  @moduledoc "Queues fake OpenAI Responses API replies for ReqLLM."

  @doc "Request options that send model calls to this module."
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 128,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    }
  end

  @doc "Queues one reply per model call, in order."
  def respond_with(bodies) do
    Enum.each(bodies, fn body ->
      Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, &Req.Test.json(&1, body))
    end)
  end

  @doc "A final text answer."
  def text(text) do
    response([
      %{
        "type" => "message",
        "id" => "msg_1",
        "role" => "assistant",
        "status" => "completed",
        "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
      }
    ])
  end

  @doc "A request to call one tool. Use a new `call_id` for each call in a request."
  def tool_call(name, arguments, call_id \\ "call_1") do
    response([
      %{
        "type" => "function_call",
        "id" => "fc_" <> call_id,
        "call_id" => call_id,
        "name" => name,
        "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
        "status" => "completed"
      }
    ])
  end

  defp response(output) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp_1",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 20,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 5,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 25
      }
    }
  end
end
```

`req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]` routes the HTTP request to
the queued replies. `api_key` is a placeholder; nothing leaves the machine.
Each reply reports 20 input and 5 output tokens. Queued replies are used once,
in order; a request that stops early leaves the rest for the next request.

## Run One Request

Queue two replies: the model first asks for `lookup_order`, then answers with
the tool result. Then build the request Signal and run it:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.tool_call("lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"}),
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text("Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02.")
])

agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, next_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

reply.type
```

The result is `"jido.ai.response"`.

`request_signal!/1` builds a `jido.ai.request` Signal. Its data is the Flow
input: `input` is required, and any other ReAct field, such as `options`,
replaces the Agent default for this request. The replacement is whole:
`MyApp.OfflineModel.options/0` sets every option the request uses. With a real provider, leave out
`options` and set the provider key that ReqLLM reads, such as
`OPENAI_API_KEY`.

`cmd/3` runs the whole ReAct graph in this process: model call, tool call,
model call. It returns the next Agent and one `Reply` Directive. It starts no
process and commits nothing.

## Read The Reply

```elixir
{reply.data.status, reply.data.output}
```

The result is
`{:completed, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02."}`.

```elixir
Map.take(reply.data, [:model_calls, :tool_rounds, :tool_calls, :reason_code])
```

The result is `%{model_calls: 2, tool_rounds: 1, tool_calls: 1, reason_code: :none}`.

| `status` | Meaning | `output` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:completed` | The Flow produced an answer. | The answer |
| `:blocked` | A guard or limit stopped the request. | `""` |
| `:failed` | The model call failed after retries and fallbacks. | `""` |

A blocked or failed reply still returns `{:ok, next_agent, [reply]}`. Read
`reason_code` and `reason` for the cause.

Usage adds up across both model calls:

```elixir
Map.take(reply.data.usage, [:input_tokens, :output_tokens, :total_tokens])
```

The result is `%{input_tokens: 40, output_tokens: 10, total_tokens: 50}`.

`reply.data.model_attempts` has one entry per model call, with content-free
Context snapshots from before and after the call.
[Inspect Model Context](inspect-model-context.livemd) reads them.

## Read The Session

The original Agent is unchanged. The next Agent holds the conversation:

```elixir
{agent.state.session, next_agent.state.session.thread.rev}
```

The result is `{nil, 8}`. The first request created a `Jido.Session` with one
`Jido.Thread`, and each Thread entry adds one revision.

```elixir
Enum.map(next_agent.state.session.thread.entries, & &1.kind)
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[:ai_message, :ai_model_attempt, :ai_message, :ai_tool_attempt, :ai_message,
 :ai_model_attempt, :ai_message, :ai_request_outcome]
```

The four `:ai_message` entries are the user message, the tool call, the tool
result, and the answer. Only those can enter the next model call. The model
attempts, the tool attempt, and the request outcome are records for you, not
prompt content.

Run a second request against `next_agent` and the model sees the first
exchange. [Sessions and Threads](sessions-and-threads.md) covers the entries.

## Run It Live

`cmd/3` returns a candidate Agent. To keep the Session across requests, give
the Agent to a `Jido.AgentServer`, which commits each request's state:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(MyApp.SupportAgent, id: "support-live")

signal = MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?"})

{:ok, committed, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(server, signal)

committed.state.session.thread.rev
```

This block calls the real provider, so it needs a provider key. It isn't part
of the offline notebook.
[Pure or Live Execution](pure-or-live-execution.md) compares the two paths.

## Summary

- `use Jido.AI.Agent` defines a Jido Agent with one reasoning Flow, a tool
  allowlist, and a `session` state field.
- Reasoning defaults are validated against the Flow's input schema at compile
  time.
- `reasoning/0`, `explain/0`, and `preflight/1` inspect the Agent without
  calling a model.
- `request_signal!/1` builds a `jido.ai.request`; `cmd/3` returns the next
  Agent and one `jido.ai.response` reply.
- The reply reports `status`, `output`, `usage`, and `model_calls`.
- The Session's Thread records every message and model attempt.

